{"gap_id": "GAP-070", "category": "EG", "priority": "P2", "status": "OPEN", "skill": "research", "section": "parameter 12, ramp run length", "description": "RISE-BASED LANDING SPACING HAS NO PARAMETER. Parameter 12 (TERM-091) holds limits stated as a horizontal run or going between landings (UK, IE, DE, ES, PT, FR, SG, NZ). Several codes state the same protection as a maximum vertical rise between landings instead, and those figures are NOT written: US ADA 2010 405.6 (30 in, REF-00987), JP (REF-00990, a rise of 75 cm per landing; not read this session), KR REF-01031 (0.75 m, read), FI REF-01017 (500 mm continuous rise before a 2000 mm landing, read), SE REF-01024 (0.5 m between landings, read), NL REF-01021 (1 m of rise per ramp, read), UG REF-01010 (3600 mm combined rise, read). Writing a rise under parameter 12 would put a vertical length beside horizontal ones in one unit family, and the engine would compose them (most accommodating = smallest figure). Observing the phrase to mint a parameter adds adjudication debt the owner rules on (OR-17). Not filed as a parameter here. Re-derive the list by searching the held texts for landing in each language."}
{"gap_id": "GAP-071", "category": "EG", "priority": "P3", "status": "OPEN", "skill": "research", "section": "term TERM-092, intermediate landing", "description": "LANDING DIMENSIONS ARE UNRECORDED AS ROWS. TERM-092 (intermediate landing) is a term but not a parameter, so the landing length and depth that the read sources state are in notes only, not extractions: DE 150 cm (REF-00988), PT 1.5 m (REF-01032, 2.5.6), ES 1.50 m (REF-01027, 4.3.3), SG 1500 mm depth (REF-00139, 4.6.5.2(a)), NZ 1200 mm minimum and 1500 mm where possible (REF-01016, 6.5.2), UG 1500 mm at top and bottom (REF-01010). Promoting TERM-092 needs add-parameter and, for a direction, a source that states one. Not done: the request was spacing, and the promotion is a vocabulary act."}
